dia/yonne-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yonne-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yonne</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yonne is a river of central France, about 290 km long, rising in the Morvan and joining the Seine at Montereau.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yopaat-bahlam-i"><span class="enc-name">Yopaat B'ahlam I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yopaat B'ahlam I was the founder of the royal dynasty of the Maya city of Yaxchilan, who according to later inscriptions took power in 359 CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/york"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/york.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">York</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">York is a historic city in North Yorkshire, England, where the Ouse and the Foss meet.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/york-factory"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/york-factory.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">York Factory</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">York Factory was a fur-trading post of the Hudson's Bay Company at the mouth of the Hayes River on Hudson Bay, in today's Manitoba.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/york-minster"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/york-minster.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">York Minster</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">York Minster is the cathedral of York in northern England and the seat of the Archbishop of York.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/york-river-virginia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/york-river-virginia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">York River (Virginia)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The York River is a tidal estuary in eastern Virginia, formed by the junction of the Pamunkey and Mattaponi rivers and flowing about 55 kilometres into Chesapeake Bay.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/york-maine"><span class="enc-name">York, Maine</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">York is a town on the southern coast of Maine, United States, settled by the English in the 1630s as Agamenticus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yorkshire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yorkshire</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yorkshire is a historic county of northern England, the largest in the country, stretching from the Pennines to the North Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire-wolds"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yorkshire-wolds.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yorkshire Wolds</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yorkshire Wolds are low chalk hills in the East Riding of Yorkshire and North Yorkshire, England, curving from the Humber to the coast at Flamborough Head.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yoruba"><span class="enc-name">Yoruba</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yoruba are a major West African people of south-western Nigeria and neighbouring Benin, with a long tradition of urban city-states and the sacred city of Ile-Ife.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yorubaland.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yorubaland</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yorubaland is the cultural homeland of the Yoruba people, covering south-western Nigeria and parts of Benin and Togo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-shoin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yoshida-shoin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yoshida Shōin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yoshida Shōin (1830–1859) was a samurai teacher and thinker of the Chōshū domain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-koriyama-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yoshida-koriyama-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yoshida-Kōriyama Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Yoshida-Kōriyama Castle was a mountain castle in Aki province, now in Akitakata in Hiroshima Prefecture, the home base of the Mōri clan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yoshino-nara"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yoshino-nara.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yoshino, Nara</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yoshino is a mountain town in Nara Prefecture, Japan, famous for the cherry trees of Mount Yoshino and for its shrines and temples on the pilgrimage routes of the Kii…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/youliu-chanyu"><span class="enc-name">Youliu Chanyu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Youliu was chanyu of the Northern Xiongnu in the late 1st century CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/young-bukharans"><span class="enc-name">Young Bukharans</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Young Bukharans were a reformist movement in the Emirate of Bukhara, influenced by the Jadid movement and the Young Turks, who sought modern education and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/young-khivans"><span class="enc-name">Young Khivans</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Young Khivans were a reformist movement in the Khanate of Khiva, formed in the 1910s on the model of the Young Turks and the Jadid reformers of Central Asia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/youth-pledge"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/youth-pledge.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Youth Pledge</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Youth Pledge (Sumpah Pemuda) was the declaration made on 28 October 1928 at the Second Indonesian Youth Congress in Batavia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/youzhou"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/youzhou.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Youzhou</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Youzhou was a historical prefecture of northern China centred on the city of Ji, on the site of modern Beijing.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yoweri Museveni</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yoweri Museveni is a Ugandan politician and former rebel leader who has been President of Uganda since 1986.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ypati"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ypati.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ypati</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ypati is a village in Phthiotis, central Greece, on the slopes of Mount Oeta above the Spercheios valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ypres"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ypres.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ypres</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ypres (Ieper) is a city in West Flanders, Belgium.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yser"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yser.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yser</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yser is a river that rises in northern France and flows through West Flanders in Belgium to the North Sea at Nieuwpoort.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yu-county-hebei"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yu-county-hebei.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yu County, Hebei</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yu County, or Yuxian, is a county in Zhangjiakou in north-western Hebei province, China, in an upland basin near the Shanxi border.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yu-deuk-gong"><span class="enc-name">Yu Deuk-gong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yu Deuk-gong was a Korean scholar of the Silhak school in the late Joseon period, who served in the royal library under King Jeongjo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yu-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yu the Great</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yu the Great is the legendary Chinese ruler said to have tamed the great flood by dredging channels rather than building dams, and to have founded the Xia dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yufir-ibn-abd-al-rahman-al-hiwali"><span class="enc-name">Yu'fir ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Hiwali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yu'fir ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Hiwali was a Yemeni chief of the Himyarite Hiwal tribe who founded the Yufirid dynasty in the 9th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuan Dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yuan Dynasty was the Mongol-ruled dynasty of China, proclaimed by Kublai Khan in 1271, which completed the conquest of the Southern Song in 1279.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuan-haowen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-haowen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuan Haowen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuan Haowen was a poet, critic and historian of the Jin dynasty from Xinzhou in Shanxi, widely regarded as the greatest poet of northern China in his age.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-shao.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuan Shao</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuan Shao was a Chinese warlord of the late Eastern Han dynasty, from a family of high officials.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuan Shikai</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuan Shikai was a Chinese general who built the modern Beiyang Army under the Qing.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-shu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuan Shu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuan Shu was a Chinese warlord of the late Eastern Han dynasty and cousin of Yuan Shao.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuasa-wakayama"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuasa-wakayama.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuasa, Wakayama</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuasa is a coastal town in Wakayama Prefecture, Japan, on the Kii Peninsula, traditionally regarded as the birthplace of Japanese soy sauce.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yucatán Peninsula</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yucatán Peninsula is a low limestone platform in south-eastern Mexico and northern Central America, separating the Gulf of Mexico from the Caribbean Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yudhishthira"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yudhishthira.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yudhishthira</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yudhishthira is the eldest of the five Pandava brothers in the Mahabharata, the son of Kunti and, in the epic, of the god Dharma.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yue-fei"><span class="enc-name">Yue Fei</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yue Fei was a Southern Song general who led campaigns against the Jurchen Jin dynasty and its client state of Qi in the 1130s, recovering territory north of the Yangtze.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yue-yi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yue-yi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yue Yi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yue Yi was a general of the Warring States period who served King Zhao of Yan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi"><span class="enc-name">Yuezhi</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yuezhi were a nomadic people who lived in the region of the Gansu corridor in north-western China in the late first millennium BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-committee"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yugoslav-committee.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yugoslav Committee</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yugoslav Committee was a political body of South Slav exiles from Austria-Hungary, formally founded in London in 1915 and led by the Croatian politician Ante Trumbić.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yugoslav Partisans</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yugoslav Partisans were the Communist-led resistance movement in occupied Yugoslavia during the Second World War, commanded by Josip Broz Tito.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yugoslavia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yugoslavia</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">Yugoslavia was a state of the South Slavs in the Balkans, proclaimed in 1918 as the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes from territories including former…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yugra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yugra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yugra</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yugra (Arabic Yura) was the medieval name for the lands and peoples of the far north-east of European Russia and north-western Siberia, around the northern Urals and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yugurs"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yugurs.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yugurs</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yugurs, also called Yellow Uyghurs, are an ethnic group of about 14,000 people living mainly in Sunan Yugur Autonomous County in Gansu, China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuhi-v-musinga"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuhi-v-musinga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuhi V Musinga</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuhi V Musinga was king of Rwanda from 1896 to 1931.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuhwa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/yuhwa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yuhwa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yuhwa is the mother of Jumong, the founder of Goguryeo, in Korean legend.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-chounu"><span class="enc-name">Yujiulü Chounu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yujiulü Chounu was khagan of the Rouran from 508 to 520.</span></a></li></ul>
